.TH GHOSTLIGHT-BROWSER-CONNECTOR 1 "2026-09-05" "Ghostlight 1.3.4" "Ghostlight Manual"
.SH NAME
ghostlight-browser-connector \- Chromium native messaging relay for Ghostlight
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ghostlight-browser-connector
.SH DESCRIPTION
The browser connector is the native messaging host Chromium starts on behalf of
the Ghostlight extension. It relays typed frames between the extension and the
local Ghostlight authority.
.PP
It takes no arguments and is not intended to be run by hand. Chromium starts it
from a native-messaging host manifest that
.BR ghostlight (1)
installs, and only for the exact allowed extension.
.PP
The connector is deliberately opaque. It does not deserialize adapter commands,
receipts, events, workspace identifiers, or presentation signals; unknown
bounded payloads round-trip unchanged. This is what lets the authority gain
capabilities without changing the relay.
.PP
If the local authority is not running, the connector starts the exact sibling
.B ghostlight
executable in its own installation directory, with no arguments, and continues
its ordinary reconnect loop.
.SH NOTES
A browser installed from Snap or Flatpak runs confined and cannot start this
program. That is a sandbox boundary, not a Ghostlight defect. Install a native
Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium package, then run
.BR "ghostlight doctor --fix" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/opt/chrome/native-messaging-hosts/org.sylin.ghostlight.json
System manifest installed by the Debian package. Equivalent paths exist for
Edge, Brave, and Chromium.
.TP
.I ~/.config/google-chrome/NativeMessagingHosts/org.sylin.ghostlight.json
Per-user manifest installed by the per-user route. Equivalent paths exist for
the other supported browsers.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ghostlight (1),
.BR ghostlight-mcp-connector (1)
